// Text of every case-study slide, taken from the Figma slide decks
// ("SwiftConnect — Portfolio slides", "Chicora (Fashion App) — Portfolio
// slides"). The slides are images, so this is what screen readers and
// search engines get instead: the headline goes into the image's alt text,
// the rest into a visually hidden description next to it.
// Each entry: [headline, body]. Keep in step with the slides when they change.

export const SC_TEXT = [
  ["SwiftConnect: the future of proactive support", "An AI-assisted workspace for support agents that removes handover delays, keeps service quality stable and turns peak-season surges into seamless customer experiences. Role: UI/UX Designer. Platform: web, agent desktop. Focus: AI-to-human handover. Research, synthesis, IA and flows, hi-fi UI, usability testing."],
  ["Peak season broke the support desk", "Bots take the first touch, but every handover to a human agent stalls. During sales the queue explodes. Business figures are from the project brief. Traffic 5×: 50,000 → 250,000 daily visitors, a 400% surge. Volume: 4,000 support tickets per day during peaks. Delay: 8 hrs response time, up from 15 min (32×). Churn +15%: rise in manual order-cancellation requests. Abandon +22%: more customers leaving due to lack of trust. The brief: design an agent workspace that stays calm under a 5× surge: bots and humans hand over without losing context, AI suggestions are trustworthy, and managers see trouble before it reaches social media. Success looks like: faster handovers, fewer tabs per ticket, AI agents trust, real-time queue visibility."],
  ["The handover is the bottleneck", "Peak traffic is the trigger, but the delay is created at one moment: when a bot hands a chat to a human who has no context. 5× traffic surge (250,000 visitors a day) → more bot escalations (4,000 tickets a day) → context lost at handover (84% of surveyed agents) → 110 s catch-up reading logs per ticket → queue backs up, agents fall behind → 8 h delays and +15% churn, customers give up. Rough estimate: 4,000 tickets × 110 s ≈ 122 hrs of agent time per peak day, if every ticket involves a handover. An estimate, not a measurement; the 110 s figure needs its source confirmed. So the design goal is: not “answer faster”. Start every chat already knowing the context."],
  ["What the market gets right, and wrong", "SWOT of four competitors. Zendesk: massive scalability, 100+ languages and thousands of integrations; but high cognitive load, a “maze of tabs” that slows agents; AI Copilot can cut manual triage by 220+ hours a month; complex setup and high pricing hold back mid-market growth. Gorgias: deep Shopify integration, agents edit orders inside the chat; limited customisation, admins stuck with preset AI intents; image-recognition AI speeds up damaged-item claims; per-ticket pricing punishes brands during peak surges. Intercom: “Fin” AI agent leads at resolving complex queries on its own; confusing, costly pay-per-resolution ($0.99) pricing; AI summaries help humans catch up on long chats in seconds; messy workflows across several brand mailboxes. Freshdesk: “Freddy AI” gives proactive insights and solid sentiment analysis; feature bloat, 36% of agents feel buried in tools and toggles; “Command Center” unifies WhatsApp, social and email in one view; reporting stays basic unless you buy the top Enterprise tier."],
  ["Four gaps SwiftConnect can own", "Tab fatigue: legacy platforms force agents through 4–6 disjointed tabs per ticket → unified architecture, one workspace with chat, IoT status and history in a single sidebar. Context drop: agents read long, messy chat logs during handovers while customers wait → executive summaries, an automatic 3-bullet briefing (issue, action, sentiment) before the chat starts. Generic response: robotic automation and templates miss technical nuance → confidence copilot, ML confidence scores with sources so agents can check an answer before sending it. Sentiment blindness: managers see a brand crisis only after the social-media backlash → live heatmapping, a stakeholder dashboard showing the emotional pulse of every queue. Summaries alone already exist (Intercom, Zendesk). SwiftConnect’s edge is combining the briefing with live device context and verified confidence, built for hardware support where generic AI fails."],
  ["Goal-directed research, not opinion polls", "We didn’t ask agents if they liked the current system. Every question targeted one of three pillars, so findings translated straight into UI requirements: cognitive load (to solve dashboard clutter), information gaps (to fix the bot-to-human handover) and trust levels (to design AI suggestions agents feel confident using). Quantitative survey measuring the “scaling strain” of peak seasons: 74 starts, 50 submissions, 67.5% completion. Who we heard from: 35 junior agents, 10 senior leads, 5 admins and managers. Average experience in technical support 2.8 years; average stress rating during peak season 9.2 / 10."],
  ["Context loss is the #1 pain", "Top pain points from 50 respondents: context loss during handover 84% (42 respondents); tab fatigue or UI clutter 76% (38); customer aggression 58% (29); inaccurate AI suggestions 24% (12). What we do about it: unified context, because 42 of 50 agents report context loss, history should be summarised before the agent joins the chat. Trust indicators: answers need visible sources before agents rely on them. Prioritisation: 58% struggle with sentiment, so the queue is colour-coded, red for frustrated and green for calm."],
  ["What an agent and a manager told us (n = 2)", "Sarah, senior support agent and admin, focus on agent burnout and information overload. On the first 30 seconds after a bot handover: “It’s pure chaos. I get a notification, and the first thing I see is a wall of text from the bot conversation.” On trusting AI: “I need to know why it thinks that’s the answer… A confidence score would be a lifesaver.” On tabs: “Checking the order status… if that data were just there in the chat sidebar, I’d save at least two minutes per ticket.” Marcus, customer support manager, focus on business strategy and brand reputation. On the #1 metric: “Reduced ‘Goodwill Credit’ spend… our agents get so overwhelmed they just give out $20 discount codes.” On big sales: “Escalation clusters. One technical glitch with a smart-hub can lead to 500 angry people on Twitter in an hour.” At a glance: “I want to see which agents are drowning.”"],
  ["Four clusters, four design requirements", "Context vacuum (handover friction: information is lost when a bot transfers to a human) → an AI-generated 3-bullet summary (issue, attempts, sentiment) before the agent joins. Tab fatigue (UI fragmentation: order history and IoT status hide in external tabs) → a unified contextual sidebar that stays visible during the chat. Trust gap (AI scepticism: agents ignore suggestions that lack transparency) → ML confidence badges and a show-source toggle on AI answers. Sentiment blindness (brand risk: managers can’t see the emotional pulse of the queue) → a stakeholder heatmap of live sentiment across all chats."],
  ["Inside an agent’s peak-season shift", "Empathy map. Thinks: “The customer is already angry and I haven’t said hello.” “I’m wasting so much time reading old logs.” “If I get this fix wrong, my manager will see it.” Feels: anxious about the growing queue, fatigued by tabs and repetitive data entry, under-equipped, defensive. Says: “I already told the bot this!” “I need a quick summary of this mess.” “Where’s the order ID? Too many tabs open.” Does: toggles between 5+ browser tabs, skim-reads and misses key details, pastes order numbers into separate search bars, apologises repeatedly while catching up. Pain: cognitive overload. Gain: contextual confidence, entering every chat already knowing the who, what and why."],
  ["The Context Vacuum", "Business: peak-season delays (15 min → 8 hrs) drive cancellations and churn. Agent: at every AI-to-human handover, agents rebuild context from long transcripts and scattered tools before they can help. How might we streamline the handover to remove redundant questions and recover lost “dead time”, so agents can focus on high-value technical resolution? Impact: 84% of surveyed agents lose context at handover (42 of 50); 110 s per ticket on catch-up reading (research notes, source to confirm); 9.2/10 average peak-season stress (survey, n = 50)."],
  ["12 ideas, one clear winner", "Ideas grouped under context vacuum, tab fatigue, trust gap and sentiment blindness, including the AI-briefing header, a contextual sidebar, key-moment highlighting, hover-peek on order IDs, a pre-filled smart intro, one-click device-log sync, confidence badging, source citation, an edit-first workflow, a live heatmap, sentiment sorting and an agent SOS button. Stakeholder dot vote (PM, lead designer, tech lead, 2 senior admins; 3 votes each), internal prioritisation, not user research. Winning idea: the AI-briefing header, a 3-bullet TL;DR pinned to the top of every new chat. Highest impact: solves handover friction, the #1 pain for 84% of agents. Efficiency gain: targets the ~110 s catch-up per transition (estimate). Feasibility: high value, medium effort, fits the existing chat flow."],
  ["One primary solution, with supporting features", "Primary: AI-briefing header (context loss, 84%, 42/50) gives the agent the case at a glance when the chat arrives. Supporting: context sidebar (tab fatigue, 76%) keeps the order and device details in view. Supporting: AI verification (inaccurate AI, 24%) lets the agent check a suggested fix before sending it. Secondary: manager heatmap (aggression, 58%, and the manager interview) serves the manager, outside the agent’s handover flow. Why the handover came first: the most frequent pain, the stakeholders’ choice in the dot vote, and feasible at medium effort (team estimate)."],
  ["Designing for the agent first, the manager second", "Primary composite persona: Overwhelmed Annie, junior support agent (tier 1), 1.5 years. “I feel like I’m starting every conversation with an apology because I’m playing catch-up.” Goal: resolve tickets quickly without asking customers to repeat themselves. Frustrations: walls of text during handovers and 5+ tabs for order data. Supporting composite persona: Data-Driven David, customer support lead and admin, 8 years. “I need to stop the fire before it spreads to social media, but I’m flying blind.” Goal: protect brand reputation and cut goodwill-refund spend. Frustration: no real-time view of queue mood or agent burnout."],
  ["A support agent’s journey through a surge", "Stages: surge arrival, ticket selection, context gathering, technical diagnosis, AI verification, resolution. The agent monitors the live queue during a 400% spike, picks a critical ticket, reads the AI transfer summary, checks the sidebar for IoT device health, reviews the AI fix and its accuracy score, and sends the verified solution. Pain points: volume, unclear priorities, long chat histories, little technical data, fear of wrong advice, delays. Emotions move from worried and stressed to confident and calmer. Opportunities: manager heatmap, colour-coded status tags, the AI briefing, the IoT sidebar, a verification modal and one-click approve and send."],
  ["One workspace, one clear path to “resolved”", "Information architecture: the SwiftConnect dashboard with queue management (live queue filtered by sentiment, priority tags), the active workspace (AI-briefing header, main chat window, unified contextual sidebar with profile, order history and IoT diagnostics), the manager’s live heatmap and settings. Task flow: a ticket arrives; if it is a bot handover, Annie accepts the chat, the AI generates a summary in about 10 s, she reads the briefing (device offline, tried reset, upset), applies an AI-suggested fix at 92% confidence when the solution is clear or checks the transcript when it isn’t, and the ticket closes. Proposed recovery: if the briefing is missing or wrong, the agent opens the full transcript and flags the summary."],
  ["First high-fidelity pass, before testing", "Phase 1 hi-fi screens of the SwiftConnect agent workspace."],
  ["Early prototype feedback", "Unverified: sample size, method and question wording must be confirmed before these figures count as evidence. AI briefing, 85%: keep, maintain the high-contrast green header; reported less reading time. IoT sidebar, 80%: add direct “Reset” actions in the sidebar; reported faster resolution. Manager map, 70%: refine with tooltips to reduce visual density; reported better visibility. Self-reported ratings on a 1–5 scale: response speed 4.7, agent confidence 4.5. 95% said the briefing reduced reading time and 90% said they felt confident in the AI verification’s technical accuracy (self-reported)."],
  ["What testing changed", "From the original test notes; method and participant count still to be confirmed. Keep: the high-contrast green briefing header (screen 03). Add: direct “Reset” actions in the sidebar (screen 04). Refine: tooltips on the manager map to reduce density (screen 06)."],
  ["A calm, accessible system for long shifts", "Design system: blue, red, teal and neutral colour scales; Poppins type scale from H1 56/68 bold to H6 20/24 bold; spacing and radius tokens. Accessibility: 4.5:1 minimum text contrast, 32 px minimum button height, 44 × 44 touch targets, and (proposed) sentiment never relies on colour alone."],
  ["The AI briefing: the case at a glance", "What the briefing shows: 01 the issue, what the customer needs, e.g. a device that’s offline; 02 already tried, the steps taken with the bot, e.g. a reset; 03 sentiment, how the customer feels, e.g. upset. Proposed: a recommended next action linked to the source messages it came from, so the agent never has to trust the summary blindly. Why it’s primary: it targets the most frequent pain, context loss at handover (84%)."],
  ["Supporting context, with the agent in control", "Interactive sidebar: keeps the order and device details the briefing refers to in view, so agents don’t switch tools. AI verification modal: the agent reviews a suggested fix and its confidence before sending. The score prompts a check; it isn’t proof."],
  ["Beyond the handover", "Secondary capabilities, outside the primary handover flow, shown for context and not validated as part of this case study: login and agent overview (entry point and shift overview), live support queue (sentiment colour-coding helps agents pick the next ticket), and the manager’s real-time heatmap (spot escalation clusters early)."],
  ["Edge cases and AI safeguards", "Proposed for the next iteration, not part of the tested prototype. New information during the handover: the briefing updates and tags anything “New since handover”. Visual noise: the sidebar collapses to the few fields the briefing references. Confidence score: a prompt to verify, not proof; high shows with its source, medium says “needs verification”, low hides the suggestion. Sarcasm and slang: treat sentiment as a hint that agents can correct. Sentiment scores: agents see a neutral priority tag; the raw score stays on the manager view. Missing or wrong summary: the full transcript is one click away, sending stays edit-first, and agents can flag the summary."],
  ["From evidence to validation", "Context loss at handover (84%, survey) → AI briefing (primary) → agent understands the case without reading the whole transcript → validate with time to understand a handover. Tab fatigue (76%) → supporting context sidebar → fewer tool switches → measure tool switches per task. Inaccurate AI suggestions (24%) → verification before sending, with sources → agents only send checked answers → errors caught in testing and trust rating. Customer aggression (58%) and the manager interview → priority tags and manager heatmap → escalations spotted earlier → manager task test and goodwill-credit spend after launch."],
  ["What we can claim, and what’s next", "What we can claim: research identified context loss at handover as the most frequent agent pain (84% of 50 surveyed); the design centres on an AI briefing with supporting context and verification; early prototype feedback was positive but self-reported. Limitations: self-reported survey data, only two qualitative interviews, usability sample size and method not confirmed, AI summary accuracy untested on real conversations, no measured operational or business impact. Next steps: a moderated test of whether agents can state the issue and next action from the briefing, time to understand a handover against today’s baseline, tracking errors and trust concerns, and after launch time to first reply, redundant questions, AI acceptance and goodwill-credit spend."],
  ["Let’s talk: want the full story?", "There’s more behind this case study than fits on slides: the trade-offs, what I’d test next, and how the design evolved. I’d love to walk you through it. In the conversation: the trade-offs behind the AI briefing, how I’d validate it with real agents, how I work with product and engineering, and what I would do differently. Open to full-time roles, interviews and freelance projects."],
];

export const FA_TEXT = [
  ["Chicora: fashion that fits, from the first tap", "A personalised, trustworthy mobile shopping app that blends style, simplicity and smart recommendations into one seamless experience. Role: UI/UX Designer. Platform: mobile app (iOS / Android). Method: design thinking, 10 interviews, personas, IA and wireframes, style guide, hi-fi UI."],
  ["Reimagine how people shop for clothes online", "A fashion-tech startup saw a saturated market where most apps fail to feel personal, engaging or trustworthy. The brief, a mobile prototype that offers: effortless discovery, personal recommendations based on style preferences, trust built in (accurate product details, sizing and reviews), social sharing, and a smooth checkout that reduces drop-offs."],
  ["Design Thinking, end to end", "Empathize: 10 user interviews and empathy maps. Define: mapped problem categories and set research goals. Ideate: AI try-on, Instagram integration, seasonal bundles, flows and journeys. Prototype: low-fi wireframes refined into hi-fi mockups with a style guide. Test: wireframe usability tests on navigation, hierarchy and expectations. Why design thinking: user-centred, iterative and collaboration-friendly."],
  ["Five questions the research had to answer", "Behaviour: understand how people shop for fashion today. Frustrations: identify what annoys users in existing apps. Personalisation and trust: explore the need for tailored, credible experiences. Social and AI: test acceptance of AI fashion tools and social features. Delight: discover what makes shopping engaging and fun."],
  ["Ten interviews, ten aligned questions", "How do you currently shop for clothes online? What’s the most frustrating part of fashion apps? Do you trust sizing and product details? How important is personalisation? Would you use an AI “try-on” feature? How do you decide whether to trust a brand or app? Do you share fashion finds with friends or on social? What would make browsing more exciting? If you could improve one thing, what would it be? Have you ever abandoned a cart, and why?"],
  ["What we heard, in three buckets", "Directional findings from 10 interviews, not universal conclusions. Needs: personalised recommendations, size accuracy, reliable reviews, trust. Pain points: too many choices, poor fit, hidden costs, difficult returns. Opportunities: AI try-on, mood-based browsing, social style boards, easy returns."],
  ["Excited to shop, afraid to commit", "Empathy map. Says: “I love scrolling, but I end up confused with so many options.” “Sizing charts are useless; I always return stuff.” Thinks: “AI try-on sounds cool, but will it be accurate?” “Returns are a nightmare, so I avoid buying online.” Does: adds items to the wishlist but rarely buys, checks reviews before every purchase, spends hours hunting for offers. Feels: overwhelmed yet excited, curious but sceptical, FOMO when trends sell out, anxious before placing an order."],
  ["Shoppers hesitate at the moment of purchase", "Shoppers can find clothes they like, but can’t tell whether an item will fit or match its photos, so they hesitate, abandon, or buy and return. Primary user: a time-pressed shopper burnt by sizing (Arjun). Key task: choose the right item and size. How might we help shoppers judge fit and quality before they buy, so they can purchase with confidence? Supporting insights: fit is a recurring doubt, returns feel risky, too much choice and too little relevance."],
  ["One primary shopper, two supporting", "Composite personas from the 10 interviews. Primary: Arjun Mehta, 32, IT professional who shops on mobile during work breaks; goal is reliable clothes without wasting time; barrier is inconsistent sizing and frequent returns; success is picking the right size the first time. Supporting: Riya Sharma, 23, college student and trendy explorer, and Neha Kulkarni, 38, family shopper who finds returns stressful."],
  ["From finding to decision to test", "Sizing doubts → size guidance beside the size selector (proposed) → validate by selecting a size with confidence. Painful returns → return and exchange terms before Add to bag (proposed) → find the return conditions. Checking reviews → rating near the price and reviews on the product page → compare two products. Choice overload → categories and filters on home and listing → find suitable items faster. Hidden costs → coupons and order total in the cart → review the order total. Hypotheses not prioritised: social sharing, a personalised feed, AI try-on and occasion bundles."],
  ["A short, predictable path from browse to buy", "Information architecture and low-fidelity wireframes: login → home → product → cart → checkout → order confirmed. Each step mirrors the designed screens."],
  ["A warm, confident style guide", "Colours: fashion pink #E91E63, midnight black, pastel blue, royal purple, success green, warm orange. Typography: Poppins, heading 1 32/40 bold to caption 12 regular. Buttons in small (32px), medium (42px) and large (52px). Pink on white is 4.3:1, so it’s for buttons and large text; small pink text uses #C2185B (5.9:1). 4-column mobile grid, Material Symbols Rounded icons."],
  ["A more confident product page", "Current and proposed product page side by side. What changes and why: 01 size guidance beside the size selector, a fit finder suggesting a size based on the size you usually buy and the brand’s size chart; 02 return and exchange terms shown before Add to bag; 03 the rating kept close to the price. Proposed, not yet tested. Fit guidance reduces doubt but never guarantees fit. Validate with tasks: select a size with confidence; find the return conditions."],
  ["Discover: from sign-in to a shortlist", "Final UI: 01 login with email or social sign-in; 02 home with categories, search and deals as starting points; 03 product listing with filters and product cards showing price and offer."],
  ["Evaluate and buy: product page and cart", "Journey checkpoints: evaluate the product (images, price, rating and size selector, in the UI); fit guidance and return terms (proposed); review the order in the cart with coupons and total (in the UI); checkout and confirmation are wireframed, hi-fi still to design."],
  ["What was tested, and what still needs proof", "Tested: wireframe usability on navigation clarity, button hierarchy and expectations (per project notes). Proposed validation plan: find a suitable product, compare two products, select a size with confidence, find the return conditions, complete the purchase, recover from a validation error; measure completion, time, errors and confidence against a baseline. Limitations: ten interviews give directional insights, fit guidance and return terms are untested, results are not documented, checkout exists only as wireframes, and there is no conversion or return-rate data yet."],
  ["Let’s talk: want the full story?", "There’s more behind this case study than fits on slides: the trade-offs, what I’d test next, and how the design evolved. In the conversation: the trade-offs behind the product page, how I’d validate the fit guidance, how I work with product and engineering, and what I would do differently. Open to full-time roles, interviews and freelance projects."],
];

