// Portfolio: the case study of this site (33 slides)
export const PF_TEXT = [
  ["Designing the Design Lab", "How I turned a portfolio into a place: an immersive, spatial website you fly into, built desktop-first on the road to a VR version. Role: concept, UX, UI, motion direction and QA. Team: me and two front-end developers, with AI pair-programming later. Timeline: March to October 2026, seven months and 249 commits. Platforms: desktop, iPad and phone, with VR in progress."],
  ["Every portfolio looks the same, and mine was selling immersive design in a flat grid", "Hiring managers, design leads and recruiters open dozens of portfolios a week. Sameness: grids and PDF decks all look alike, and craft only shows three clicks deep. Little time: anything that slows a reviewer down, like a long intro, a hunt for the resume or a dead link, costs the interview. Credibility gap: showing spatial and AI-assisted work as flat thumbnails undercuts the claim. How might I let a hiring manager feel how I design spatial experiences, in under a minute, without making them work for it?"],
  ["The goal is a VR Design Lab you can walk into", "The long-term vision: put on a headset, step into a lab, and the work sits on spatial panels around you, with an AI guide, Nova, who knows every project and talks by voice. Why it couldn't ship first: most reviewers open a link on a laptop or phone, not a headset; it's a long build before anyone sees a case study; and there is a real risk of a gimmick that hides the work."],
  ["Ship an immersive desktop version first", "Keep from VR: a place, not a page; spatial HUD panels with depth, glass and light; Nova beside the work; diegetic motion where the camera travels and panels fly in. Adapt for the browser: scroll drives the flight, two HUDs (work on the left, Nova on the right), mouse, keyboard and touch instead of controllers, one panel at a time on phones. The payoff: it works in any browser today, reviewers can give real feedback, and the HUD components and content carry over to VR."],
  ["What “good” had to mean for a hiring manager", "Six criteria: fast to the work (a case study three clicks from arrival); never trapped (the cinematic can be skipped at any moment, every screen has a way back); resume one click away; works everywhere (desktop, iPad and phone, no cut-down mobile site); accessible (keyboard, screen reader, reduced motion, no text under 12 px); evidence that holds up (case studies readable as text and linkable slide by slide)."],
  ["It started on paper, on 17 March", "My kickoff notes from the first story draft, a week before the first commit. Items needed: domain and hosting, data for the AI, finalised screens, and documentation. Nova's brief from the same page: collect the visitor's basic requirements and contact details, tell the story behind the solutions rather than just listing projects, and be very direct and polite with clear, short answers."],
  ["Studying the sites that make people stop scrolling", "The mood board of award-winning immersive sites: Active Theory, Bruno Simon, Lusion, Robin Payot, Atmos, The Spark, The Year of Greta, Choo-Choo World, Delassus and the Donprod loader on Awwwards. What I took: scroll as storytelling (the scroll-driven arrival), a portfolio as a place (the lab and its HUDs), and polish while waiting (a loader with a real percentage). What I left out: game-style controls and hidden navigation, because a reviewer with five minutes shouldn't have to learn a UI."],
  ["Seven months, 249 commits, five phases", "Commits per month from the git history: March 4 (kickoff, React build), April 22 (intro animation, Nova chat with voice), June 11 (project pages, resizable chat, tooltips), July 40 (round 1 design QA), August 11 (round 2, mobile drawer), September 15 (responsive), October 146 (HUD redesign, case studies, go live, audit and re-tests). Phases: concept and Figma drafts, build 1, design QA rounds, redesign and launch, audit and re-test loop."],
  ["A story you scroll through, with exits everywhere", "Two versions of the first lo-fi user flow. An onboarding layer (scroll down to begin your journey), entering the lab with light, sound and motion cues, a prompt inside the pod to continue the story with the work and the AI one step away, a focus mode that dims nearby sections, a quick-access menu that is always visible, and three end points: narrative conclusion, project details or an AI conversation."],
  ["The map: one hub, three paths, AI as a helper", "The information architecture from the story draft: cinematic intro, onboarding, entering the lab, the pod as the main hub, then a first decision layer with story mode, View My Work and an optional AI assistant, global quick access and system guidance, a focus system, and three end states. Kept to launch: the entry sequence, quick access and micro-hints, the dimming focus system, and an AI that helps but never replaces navigation. Changed: story mode became the About Me tab, the project grid became category cards and project tabs, and the narrative conclusion became the Let's talk slide."],
  ["The first concept made you choose: AI mode or Web mode", "Draft 0, the earliest desktop flow: the welcome screen offered Talk with my AI Assistant or Jump on Web Mode. AI mode was a full chat window where work slid in on the left as the chat shrank, with a drag handle like Chrome's split view. Web mode had category cards, project pages and Nova as a drawer from the right. Why it changed: two modes meant two products and a decision before seeing anything, so Draft 1 merged them into one lab with the work on the left HUD and Nova on the right. The split handle and slide-in panels survived."],
  ["The arrival is the first impression", "A scroll-driven flight from the clouds, through a rain-soaked city, into a pod, then into the lab. Clouds: a glass scroll hint. City: speed, scale and a progress bar. Pod: the doors open. Lab: the HUD panels fly in. Scroll instead of autoplay so the visitor controls the pace; scrolling back reverses the flight, and Skip the cinematic is visible on every frame."],
  ["The arrival, scripted frame by frame", "The story draft named exact frames. Frame 0 with a scroll hint shipped as a glass card. Sound muted by default, so nobody is embarrassed opening the site in an office, shipped as soft interface ticks only with a remembered mute switch. Frame 10 stopped the scroll with three options (talk to my AI, continue the story, jump to Web mode); this was replaced by free scrolling and Skip on every frame. Frame 227 opened the door with a glitch-in HUD; the HUDs now fly in on the last frame. Ten-second floating hints became first-visit hints, and focus mode became the dimming room."],
  ["HUDs that behave like objects in a room", "The Figma HUD shape library: left HUD in small, medium and large, right (Nova) HUD variants, extended screens, icon states and tabs. Principles: two zones with two jobs (work left, Nova right); cut corners rather than rounded cards, borrowed from cockpit displays; dark liquid glass so the scene stays visible; the room dims when a panel takes focus and a slow drone hover keeps it alive at rest; 1 px light edges that stay crisp at any screen scale."],
  ["Six states, so the AI always shows what it's doing", "Nova's orb has six designed states: idle, welcome, listening, thinking, speaking and searching. Idle breathes on the home screen, welcome greets you, listening reacts to your voice and searching runs while Nova pulls up work. Five ship as video loops (idle, welcome, listening, searching, speaking); thinking was folded into searching. An AI that gives no feedback feels broken, so the states answer did it hear me before the answer arrives."],
  ["The first complete flow, designed before any code", "Draft 1 in Figma: arrival, lab home with Work and About tabs, four category cards, Nova with starter chips and a first-visit tooltip, and the project view. What worked: the two-HUD layout, the arrival and Nova's starter chips all survived to launch. What changed later: placeholder copy, categories with nothing behind them, one generic welcome line and no chapter navigation."],
  ["Build 1 didn't match the design, so I annotated every gap", "In July the dev build was reviewed side by side with Figma in a Round 1 Corrections sheet, with every difference called out and tracked until fixed: glass too transparent (add blur and drop shadow), HUD too tall, green border not as designed, card height, button and tooltip style, tab radius and Video tab width, missing home icon, reverse-scroll icon, and a blank frame after the door."],
  ["Phones aren't a shrunken lab", "Draft 2 for phones and tablets keeps the same panels but shows one at a time, and Nova moves into a drawer behind a green handle. After testing, slide text was unreadable on phones, so the phone frames gained a chapter list, pinch-zoom and a Read as text view."],
  ["From panels on a video to a lab you're standing in", "Draft 3, October: new HUD shapes from Figma, dark liquid glass, a dimming room and stronger hierarchy. Before: flat panels and generic tabs, with the welcome text and category labels competing. After: chamfered HUDs, liquid glass, a role line under the name and one clear action. Why: in review the build still read as a website over a video; the new shapes and glass give each panel weight, and dimming the room pulls focus to the work."],
  ["Shallow by design: three clicks to any case study", "The shipped information architecture: entry (loader, then Enter), the skippable cinematic, the lab home (Work and About Me), four categories (UI/UX, Social Media, Videos with an honest empty state, Immersive), project tabs (SwiftConnect, Chicora, Portfolio), slides with chapters, full screen, zoom and read as text, and a closing Let's talk slide with live buttons. Always there: Nova, the resume, social links, sound on/off and home. Every project and slide has its own shareable address, and unknown addresses get a 404 page."],
  ["Small details that make the lab feel engineered", "A techno cursor with states for normal, button, card, tab, text field, drag and press; a guided cursor that glides to Download Resume after the intro is skipped; a visible green keyboard focus ring; hover sound with a mute switch; the room dims when a panel takes focus; panels fly in on the last intro frame; chat starter chips so nobody has to think of a question."],
  ["An intro that never stands between the reviewer and the work", "Loader first with a real percentage; Enter the Design Lab, one deliberate click or Enter that unlocks sound; Skip the cinematic on every frame with an eased fast-forward. After 6 seconds the loader offers Skip the intro on slow connections; Esc and arrow keys control the intro, slides and full screen; swipe drives the intro on touch screens; reduced motion is respected."],
  ["Launched on Oct 12. Three days later, a full UX audit", "I audited the live build on desktop and phone, with screen-reader and automated accessibility checks: 22 findings, 6 critical, 6 high and 10 medium or low. The critical ones: Download Resume served an internal corrections PDF; Nova pointed at a developer's local server and showed Failed to fetch; the tab title was React App with no share preview; LinkedIn linked to the homepage; the Let's talk call to action was part of an image; slide text on phones shrank to 4–5 px."],
  ["Nielsen's 10 heuristics, scored on the live site", "Visibility of system status: good. Match with the real world: good. User control and freedom: good, mute missing. Consistency and standards: fair (Video vs Videos, two welcome texts, placeholders). Error prevention: fair (wrong resume, broken LinkedIn link). Recognition rather than recall: fair (27 slides with only dots). Flexibility and efficiency: fair (no deep links). Aesthetic and minimalist design: strong. Helping users recover from errors: poor (raw Failed to fetch, no 404). Help and documentation: good. Every fair or poor row became a ticket."],
  ["Fixing what the audit found", "Live Draft 1 fixed every critical finding. Nova now answers from built-in knowledge when the server is unreachable and never shows a raw error. A chapter list (for example 05 / 27, Gap Analysis) jumps to any part of a case study. A 404 page, This room isn't part of the lab, leads back. Honest empty states replace placeholder projects. Plus the real resume, the profile link and a live Let's talk call to action."],
  ["Accessibility, search and performance", "Accessibility: alt text and a full text description for every slide, Read as text and pinch-zoom, landmarks, one h1 per page, correct tab roles, a logical keyboard path, no text under 12 px, sound on/off, reduced motion. Search and sharing: real title, description, icon and share image, shareable links for every project and slide, readable case-study text, a 404 page. Performance: code splitting, about 45 MB of unused files removed, Skip the intro after 6 seconds, optional privacy-friendly analytics."],
  ["The same lab on a phone and an iPad", "Not a separate mobile site: the layout reflows, the intro plays with a swipe, and case studies stay readable. Phone: one HUD, Nova behind the green handle, case studies with a chapter menu, zoom and full screen. iPad landscape: a touch layout with bigger targets and no hover-only actions. Checked at 360 and 390 px phones, 768 px tablets and 1024 px landscape, including the keyboard open and Safari toolbars."],
  ["Launch was the midpoint, not the finish", "After Live Draft 1 I kept testing the real site and shipped small releases from October 21 to 27. The lab window felt flat: a 3D lab window and a camera that travels through the room. Long slide copy was hard to scan: a readability pass. HUD outlines blurred at some zoom levels: crisp 1 px edges. The screen flickered as the HUDs flew in: no backdrop blur until they land. First-time visitors missed Nova: clearer hints. The scene felt static at rest: a slow drone hover and a soft light glow. Also an intro entry card and criss-cross rain."],
  ["What I tried, what I kept, what I killed", "Merged: two doors, AI mode or Web mode, became one lab with both HUDs. Replaced: stopping after one scroll with three options became free scrolling with Skip. Changed: muted by default became soft interface ticks with a remembered mute switch. Kept: rain on the city frames, a 3D lab window and camera travel. Killed: motion blur while scrolling (smeared the frames), frame blending (ghosting), depth parallax in three approaches (stretching and tearing broke the immersion). Replaced: a breathing zoom at rest became a drone hover with a light glow. Rejected: AI-regenerated intro footage that looked toy-like. Parked: swapping in new footage; the original draft stayed."],
  ["Why depth parallax didn't ship", "The idea: split the intro into layers (clouds, towers, pod) that move at different speeds with the cursor. Three approaches: AI depth maps displacing each frame, cut-out layers, and hand-masked PSD layers with an edited background. Edge artifacts showed towers stretching and tearing where layers separated. The lesson: judge the experience, not the effect. Even the cleanest version made the pod and towers feel less real, so it was removed, keeping only a subtle background move."],
  ["A small system, applied everywhere", "Colour: lab green #00CD1F for actions, focus and progress; hover green #14E033; void #0A0A0A; panel #1B1B1B; muted #8C8C8C. Glass: white at 4–8% over the scene, 14–20 px backdrop blur, a 1 px edge at 8–12% white that turns green when active; green used for no more than about 20% of a screen. Type: Montserrat, 600–700 for the name and headings, 400–500 for body, 12 px minimum. Components: left HUD S/M/L, right HUD, tabs, category card, chip, text field, tooltip, slide frame, drawer handle."],
  ["What shipped, and where it goes from here", "Verifiable outcomes: 249 commits over seven months; 22 audit findings with all six critical ones fixed in Live Draft 1; three case studies readable as text and linkable by slide; three form factors; about 45 MB of unused files removed; no dead ends. Next: a WebXR VR Design Lab reusing the HUD components; moderated hiring-manager testing with tasks like finding a case study and the resume in under a minute; measuring resume requests with opt-in analytics; connecting Nova's live AI server with the offline answers as a fallback."],
  ["Let’s talk: want the full story?", "This site is its own case study. In the conversation: why desktop first and the plan for VR, how the audit changed the product, the experiments I killed and why, and how I work with developers and AI tools. Open to full-time roles, interviews and freelance projects."],
];
